"""scenecut_gui.py -- Scene Cut: find shot boundaries and slice a video at them.

Ported from enoky's SceneCutGUI (https://github.com/enoky/SceneCutGUI, MIT).
Upstream is one script that owns its own tk.Tk; here it is a ttk.Frame like
every other stage, so it can be a page in the Bonus tab as readily as the sole
child of its own window (see main() at the bottom).

Why it is in Studio at all
--------------------------
Everything downstream works on clips, not features: DepthCrafter, Splatting and
Inpainting all chunk a clip and assume its content is continuous. A hard cut in
the middle of a chunk gives the depth model two unrelated scenes to reconcile
and the inpainter a fill that changes halfway through. Cutting at the shot
boundaries first is what makes the rest of the pipeline behave, so a finished
split records itself for handoff and DepthCrafter can import the clips folder
without retyping it.

What changed from upstream, and why
----------------------------------
  * The detector and ffmpeg half moved to core/scenecut/detection.py, and the
    DINOv3 validator to core/scenecut/cut_validator.py. What is left here is
    the window.
  * Settings go to config_scenecut.json at the repo root, beside the other
    stages' config files, rather than a config.json in the working directory.
  * Tooltips come from dependency/scenecut_help.json through the shared
    Tooltip widget, so Studio's beginner mode can rewrite them the way it does
    for every other stage. Upstream's private _ToolTip class is gone.
  * Dialogs raised by the worker thread are queued and shown by the polling
    loop on the main thread instead. Standalone, calling into tk from a worker
    mostly got away with it; in Studio that worker shares a toplevel with four
    other stages, and one bad call takes all of them down.
  * Model handles are droppable: the shell asks stages to release the GPU
    before another one loads, and this tool can hold AutoShot, TransNetV2,
    OmniShotCut and DINOv3 at once.
  * The controls are CustomTkinter, not ttk, following DepthCrafter and both
    Inpainting stages. CTk ignores ttk.Style entirely, so every widget is
    registered as it is built and repainted by hand from _apply_ctk_palette;
    core.ui.ctk_theme carries that machinery and the two widgets CTk does not
    ship, a LabelFrame (_section) and a Spinbox (_ctk_spin). The frame around
    them is still a ttk.Frame, because that is what the shell packs.

Standalone launch keeps its own coloured console, its own window and its own
theme, exactly as it had.
"""

